Recommended slide after this architecture

Architecture Design Principles & Key Decisions

1. Oracle decoupling

Oracle is removed from the high-volume logging and historical-data persistence path.

Business transactions that genuinely require transactional DB lookups must be assessed separately before complete Oracle retirement.


2. IBM MQ → Kafka decoupling

IBM MQ remains the enterprise messaging entry point.

Kafka provides a scalable buffer, replay capability and multiple-consumer model for downstream processing.


3. On-premises object storage

Transaction/logging payloads are stored in SBI-controlled on-premises object storage using an S3-compatible API.

No public-cloud dependency.


4. Iceberg + Parquet

Parquet is the physical columnar data-file format.

Apache Iceberg provides the table/metadata layer, including ACID commits, schema evolution and partition management.

Iceberg is not another physical storage system.


5. Scalable analytics

Trino/Spark can query the same historical data without placing analytical workload on an OLTP database.

Analytics and AI workloads are therefore isolated from transaction processing.


6. High-volume protection

Kafka provides buffering when downstream storage/processing is temporarily slower than the incoming event rate.

Consumer throttling and controlled batching prevent excessive downstream database/storage concurrency.


7. Data retention strategy

Short-term operational data can remain in the appropriate operational store where required.

Large historical payloads are progressively retained in object storage rather than expensive transactional database storage.


8. Security & governance

All components remain within SBI-controlled data centres.

Access control, encryption, audit, retention and DR policies must comply with SBI security standards.



