But I would make one important change to your current diagram

Your current end-to-end flow says:

> Kafka → Object Storage → Iceberg → Parquet



This can make an architect think that these are four sequential storage steps.

That's not technically the best representation.

The better wording is:

> Kafka → On-Prem Object Storage (Parquet files) ← managed by Apache Iceberg → Trino/Spark



So on the slide, I would describe it as:

On-Prem Object Storage
Physical storage for Parquet files

Apache Iceberg
Table & metadata management layer over those files

Then:

Trino / Spark → Analytics / AI

One more point I would definitely add

Because you previously mentioned DigiLocker and Supervisory Override, don't claim that this architecture automatically eliminates all Oracle dependency.

Use this wording:

> “Oracle removal from the high-volume logging persistence path; remaining business-critical DB-dependent services will be evaluated separately for alternative transactional persistence.”



That is much more defensible in front of a Senior Architect / SBI Architecture Review Board than simply saying “Oracle Removed.”

In fact, if this is your 2-week recommendation, I would label the slide:

Target-State Direction — Oracle Decoupling & Data-Lake Architecture

rather than presenting complete Oracle elimination as something that can be delivered in two weeks.

Two-week scope: MQ/Kafka buffering, batching, logging decoupling, object-storage/Iceberg PoC/design, and workload validation.

Long-term scope: migration of genuine Oracle-dependent business transactions to an appropriate transactional database/platform.

That distinction will make your architecture much harder to challenge.
